# Domain 01: Destructive Conflict Patterns (DCP)

**The WENI Framework**

---

## Overview

Destructive Conflict Patterns (DCP) measure how individuals respond to conflict across four fundamental domains.

These patterns are **not personality traits**.  
They are **stress-response capacities** that activate under pressure.

**Key insight:**  
No pattern is good or bad in itself.  
Both deficiency and excess are pathological.

The goal is **contextually appropriate capacity**.

---

## The Four Patterns

### W — Withdraw

**What it measures:** Capacity to disengage from conflict

| Tier | Score | Label        | Description |
|-----|-------|--------------|-------------|
| Lo  | 0–20  | Insufficient | Cannot disengage; pursues relentlessly; no self-regulation |
| Med | 20–40 | Appropriate  | Takes space when needed; returns and repairs |
| Hi  | 40–60| Excess       | Shuts down; disappears; dissociates |

#### Hi Manifestations
- Leaves room during conflict
- Goes monosyllabic (“fine,” “whatever”)
- Turns away physically
- Avoids eye contact
- Dissociates / glazes over
- Uses sleep, work, or phone to avoid
- Silent treatment for hours or days
- Pretends conflict did not occur

#### Lo Manifestations
- Cannot tolerate partner’s need for space
- Follows partner room-to-room
- Panics when partner disengages
- Cannot self-soothe without reassurance
- Experiences withdrawal as abandonment
- Escalates to prevent separation

---

### E — Escalate

**What it measures:** Capacity to intensify or pursue in conflict

| Tier | Score | Label        | Description |
|-----|-------|--------------|-------------|
| Lo  | 0–20  | Insufficient | Cannot advocate; suppresses; builds resentment |
| Med | 20–40 | Appropriate  | Advocates firmly without attacking |
| Hi  | 40–60| Excess       | Pursues, attacks, cannot de-escalate |

#### Hi Manifestations
- Raises voice / yells
- Interrupts repeatedly
- Brings up past grievances as weapons
- Makes threats (“maybe we should end this”)
- Name-calling or insults
- Follows partner who tries to disengage
- Door slamming, throwing objects
- Text-bombing when partner disengages
- Catastrophizing (“you always…”)

#### Lo Manifestations
- Avoids raising concerns
- Agrees to end conflict while disagreeing
- Builds silent resentment
- Explosive delayed outbursts
- Partner doesn’t know true position
- “I’m fine” when not fine

---

### N — Negative Interpret

**What it measures:** Capacity to read intent and assign meaning

| Tier | Score | Label        | Description |
|-----|-------|--------------|-------------|
| Lo  | 0–20  | Insufficient | Over-trusts; explains away red flags |
| Med | 20–40 | Appropriate  | Reads intent accurately; gives benefit of doubt |
| Hi  | 40–60| Excess       | Mind-reads negatively; assumes malice |

#### Hi Manifestations
- “You did that on purpose”
- “You always / you never…”
- Mind-reading hostile intent
- Connecting unrelated past grievances
- Treating neutral affect as hostile
- Assuming deception without evidence
- Rejecting benign explanations

#### Lo Manifestations
- Makes excuses for harmful behavior
- Misses or minimizes red flags
- Remains in damaging situations
- Others see problems first
- Blindsided by predictable betrayal

---

### I — Invalidate

**What it measures:** Capacity to hold boundaries against partner’s experience

| Tier | Score | Label        | Description |
|-----|-------|--------------|-------------|
| Lo  | 0–20  | Insufficient | Over-validates; loses self |
| Med | 20–40 | Appropriate  | Validates while holding own perspective |
| Hi  | 40–60| Excess       | Dismisses, minimizes, corrects experience |

#### Hi Manifestations
- Eye-rolling, sighing
- “You’re overreacting”
- “That’s not what happened”
- “You’re too sensitive”
- Laughing at partner’s pain
- Correcting partner’s memory
- Walking away mid-sentence

#### Lo Manifestations
- Cannot hold own opinion
- Reflexive capitulation
- Loses preferences and agency
- Becomes emotional chameleon
- Burns out from over-empathizing
- Chronic indecision

